rchestrator. Worktree left clean, tree still equals the candidate.\n\n## What I did, аро\n\n**Reapply integrity first.** Rev 2 claims to be the rev-1 accepted work reapplied. I checked instead of trusting: applied `BUG-260902-beqfwr_accepted-work.patch` onto a clean extraction of checkpoint `6f77719` **outside any git work tree** (a `git apply` from inside the repo silently no-op'd — worth knowing) and diffed. All five code files + `README.md` byte-identical. Only `LOGBOOK.md` differs, and that diff is exactly the stale-SHA correction the rev-1 review asked for. No drift.\n\n**Gates, all rerun here:** gofmt/build/vet/test/cover/tracecheck/tracecheck -section 6.3/cataloggen -check → all 0. Coverage `internal/config` 93.7% → 93.9%, where I measured the 93.7% myself at the checkpoint rather than quoting it.\n\n**The attack that actually matters.** Reading the tables proves nothing about what they let through, so I enumerated the admitted set: 6,710 generated argv through the real `loadConfigDocument`, 260 admitted, then replayed **all 260 against live OpenSSH 10.2p1** and diffed the resolved `ssh -G` output. The admitted set moves only the declared surface; `stricthostkeychecking` only ever goes `ask → true`; **0 divergences across 16 dangerous keys** (proxycommand, localcommand, knownhostscommand, userknownhostsfile, forwardagent, …). That's a measured result, not an inference.\n\n**Twelve single-clause narrowing mutants** of my own, no delete-only ones — each reddens cases naming its own clause. M3 (kill group walking) reintroduces the exact reported `-vo` bypass and takes down 61 nodes.\n\n## One finding, recorded not blocking\n\nThe `-o` registry has a both-directions key-set pin (`permittedSSHOptionSamples`) — that's why M4–M7 can't widen it quietly. The **short-flag tables have no analogue**. I confirmed with three more mutants that the whole suite passes **green** when you permit `-E` (arbitrary file write), `-L` (port forwarding), or `-A` (agent forwarding — the exact thing `-a` is permitted to disable). The derived per-letter test *skips* permitted letters, so permitting one deletes its subtest instead of failing it.\n\nNot rework, and here's the honest reason: those are capability widenings, not §6.3 host-authentication bypasses, the production gate refuses all of them correctly today (verified in the 260-argv replay), and the two letters that *can* reach this leaf's normative class — `-F` and `-J` — both carry explicit named assertions that do redden. The fix is the existing pattern applied to a second pair of tables, and it belongs in the next ssh_args leaf, not in a reapply revision.\n\nNo `commit_ack` supplied. Scope for the commit-owning mover is the single signed commit `bc3ed70` past `6f77719`.","ttft_ms":2711,"type":"result","duration_ms":699453,"uuid":"4873f133-5986-490f-b2b4-80d174565b02","ttft_stream_ms":2199,"time_to_request_ms":16,"queued_turn_count":0}
